re]], and inventiveness. 

Estuaria is known for its low elevation and flat topography, with only about 50% of its land exceeding 1 m (3.3 ft) above sea level, and nearly 26% falling below sea level. Most of the areas below sea level, known as {{W|polders}}, are the result of {{W|land reclamation}} that began in the 18th century. Colloquially or informally, Estuaria is occasionally referred to by the ''{{W|pars pro toto}}'' [[Nova Marmoca]].

[[Politics of Estuaria|Estuarian political culture]] mostly differs on a [[Estuarian nationalism|nationalist]]-vs-confederalist continuum, rather than a left-vs-right continuum. Debates regarding Estuarian identity have played a large role in politics. Estuarian society's cohesion and specificity is based on three of its unique statutory documents: the [[Estuarian Charter of Human Rights and Freedoms]], the [[Charter of the Creole Language]], and the [[Humanitarian Decleration of Estuaria|Humanitarian Declaration (1863)]]. Until the early 1880s, [[Catharism]] played a large role in the social and cultural institutions in Estuaria. The country has a tradition of {{W|pillarisation}} and a long record of social tolerance, having legalised {{W|abortion}}, {{W|prostitution}} and {{W|human euthanasia}}, along with maintaining a liberal drug policy. Estuaria has never permitted {{W|capital punishment}}, Estuarian regions rank among the highest in national indices of {{W|press freedom}}, economic freedom, {{W|human development}} and {{W|quality of life}}. 

Estuaria's official language is {{W|Catalan language|Volisanian}}; [[Estuarian Volisanian]] is the regional variety, while there are several {{W|creole languages}}, most notably [[Estuarian Creole]]. The economy of Estuaria is mainly supported by its large service sector and varied industrial sector. For exports, it leans on these key industries: aeronautics, hydroelectricity, oil, pharmaceuticals, aluminum and paper. Estuaria is well known for producing {{W|roses}}, {{W|sugar cane}} and {{W|sunflowers}}, for its comedy, and for making {{W|rugby}} one of the most popular sports in Balisca. It is also renowned for its culture; the community has produced significant works in literature, music, films, TV shows, festivals, folklore, and more.
==History==

=== Pre-Illypnian arrival ===

=== Illypnian exploration ===
The first European explorers to visit Estuaria came in 1500 when an [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisanian]] expedition led by [[Xenokrates Lizoain]] located the opening of the [[Garrigàs Gulf]], which they subsequently explored. Natives followed the boats in large canoes, shooting arrows at the soldiers for days on end as they drifted through their territory. The Aragonese, whose crossbows had long ceased working, had no effective offensive weapons on the water and were forced to rely on their remaining armor and sleeping mats to block the arrows. About 11 soldiers were killed along this leg of the expedition, and many more wounded. Neither of the explorations made any claims to the territory for Volisania.

==== Illypnian colonization ====
{{Main|Colonial Balisca}}
{{See also|Reapers' War}}

Because Estuaria was not home to larger civilizations like the [[W:Aztec|Aztec]] and the [[W:Inca|Inca]] in [[Mexico]] and [[W:Peru|Peru]], the Aragonese could not place themselves on an established social structure. This, coupled with the fact that tangible material wealth was not found until the 18th century, made the relationship between the Aragonese and the Estuarian colony very different from the relationship of the Castilians to their possessions in the Americas. For example, the Estuarian colony was at first thought of as a commercial asset that would serve as a springboard for the Aragonese to expand trade to India (thus circumventing the Portuguese) and not a place to be settled to develop a society.

The social model of colonization in Estuaria was one geared toward commerce and entrepreneurial ideals rather than conquest as was the case in the Castilian realm. As time progressed, the Aragonese Crown found that having the colony serve as a trading post was not ideal for regulating land claims in the Americas (especially against the more established Castilians and Portuguese), so it decided that the best way to keep control of their land was to settle it. Thus, the Crown resorted to shipping ''[[W:Lançados|expulsats]]'' ({{Literal translation|the expelled}}) to solidify Aragonese land claims. Most ''expulsats'' were common criminals, although many were political or religious prisoners (e.g. 'backsliding' [[W:New Christians|New Christians]], Muslims, Jews and heretical [[Catharism|Cathars]]), who had been sentenced to be exiled from the Crown of Aragon. The sentence was not always direct - many had been given long sentences of imprisonment (sometimes death), but took the option to have their sentences commuted to a shorter period of exile overseas, in service of the crown.

''Expulsats'' played an important role in the era of Aragonese discoveries and were of outsized importance in the establishment of Aragonese colonies overseas. Eventually, most ''expulsats'' would be dropped off at a colony or (especially in the early years) abandoned on an unfamiliar shore, where they would remain for the duration of their sentence. Many were given specific instructions on behalf of the crown, and if they fulfilled them well, might earn them commutation or pardon. Common instructions included helping establish staging posts and warehouses, serving as laborers in a new colony, or garrisoning a fledgling fort. ''Expulsats'' abandoned on unfamiliar shores were often instructed to conduct exploratory work inland, searching for rumored cities, making contact with unknown peoples. In [[Algarves|the Algarves]] (in which most [[W:Crypto-Islam|crypto-Muslim]] ([[W:Morisco|Morisco]]) ''expulsats'' were shipped), these individuals became known as ''tarjumán'' ({{Literal translation|translator}}).

While many ''expulsats'' performed well enough to have their sentences reduced or pardoned as a reward, just as many (if not more) simply ignored the terms of their exile. Some jumped ship along the way, usually at a relatively safe port, rather than allowing themselves to be dropped off at some distant and dangerous shore. Others sneaked onto ships returning to the Iberian Peninsula (or some other European country) at their first chance. Some went off and formed 'outlaw' ''expulsats'' colonies, away from the supervisory eye of crown officials. Others 'went native', building up a new life of their own among the local inhabitants, abandoning their past altogether.
[[File:Ruínas_1.jpg|left|thumb|The ruins of the Cathar mission at [[Sant Domènec de les Missions]], [[Mesopotàmia]]]]
The settlements established by the Cathars were unique in that they were almost universally secular and remained autonomous and isolated from Spanish colonists and Spanish rule. The Cathars ultimately created a "[[W:State within a state|state within a state]]", and a major factor attracting the natives to the Cathars reductions was the protection they afforded from enslavement (Cathars strongly condemned the institution of [[W:Slavery|slavery]]) and the forced labour of ''[[W:Encomienda|encomiendas]],'' along with the ability to continue their traditional lifestyles relatively unrestricted.

Tensions between the Cathars and the Spanish Catholics would eventually escalate into the [[Reapers' War]]. The war's origins lay in the complex interplay of colonial expansion and [[W:Religious intolerance|religious intolerance]]. The Spanish, seeking to consolidate their control over [[Bayara]] and exploit its resources, viewed the Cathar communities with suspicion. The Cathars, practicing a [[W:Catharism#Suppression|long suppressed]] form of Christianity, were labeled as [[W:Heretics|heretics]] by the [[W:Catholic Church|Catholic Church]], providing a convenient justification for their persecution. The war itself was triggered by a dispute over land rights, but quickly escalated into a broader campaign of [[W:Religious violence|religious violence]]. Spanish militias, often motivated by the promise of land grants and religious zeal, targeted Cathar settlements and [[W:Reductions|reductions]], destroying villages and enslaving many of the indigenous residents they captured. The conflict resulted in the significant migration of Aranese and Catalan-speaking Cathars southward, fleeing the [[W:Religious persecution|religious persecution]] and [[W:Forced expulsion|forced expulsion]] from the Estuarian wetlands. These refugees predominantly settled along the west coast of Tinguiririca, establishing the [[Cerdanya|Colony of Cerdanya]]. The term ''Rodamóns'' ({{Literal translation|wanderer|vagabond}}) emerged during this period, initially used by the Spanish with a negative, classist connotation to describe the often impoverished Aranese-speaking Cathar refugees subjected to massacres and forced expulsions.

