==Government and politics==
{{Main|Government of Balisca}}

The basic structure, duties, function, and operations of the government of the republic are defined and established by the [[Basic Laws of Estuaria]] which establishes the basic law of the state and guarantees various rights and freedoms of the people. The republic's government consists of three separate branches: judicial, executive, and legislative. The legislature enacts bills, which become law following the review of the [[Constitution Council (Estuaria)|Constitucional Council]], which consists of a seven member associate justices. The regional legislature is the unicameral [[General Junta (Estuaria)|General Junta]] (known as the ''Junta estuarí'').

Estuaria is divided into four communities ([[regions of Balisca|regions]]), which are further divided into 673 ''comarques'' (cantons). There are 8,997 communes in Estuaria, with each run by an {{W|council democracy|directly elected communal council}} every five years in local elections, while every delegate is subject to instant recall. The primary revenue sources are taxes, such as [[W:Gas tax|gas]], {{W|corporate tax|corporate}} and {{W|carbon tax}}es. The Estuarian cantons do not impose a [[W:Personal income tax|personal income tax]]. The primary revenue source for comarques and municipalities is {{W|sales tax}}.
{{Align|center|
{{Estuarian regions}}
}}

===Law===
Estuaria, like the remainder of Balisca, uses a {{W|Civil law (legal system)|civil law legal system}}; that is, law arises primarily from written statutes; judges are not to make law, but merely to interpret it (though the amount of judicial interpretation in certain areas makes it equivalent to case law). In agreement with the principles of the [[Declaration of the Rights of Man]], law should only prohibit actions detrimental to society. That is, Law should lay out prohibitions only if they are needed, and if the inconveniences caused by this restriction do not exceed the inconveniences that the prohibition is supposed to remedy. Every Estuarian law must be published in the ''Diari Oficial d'Estuària'' in order for it to be considered legal and valid. This requirement was introduced in 1999, and is a part of the checks and balances system.

Estuaria does not recognize [[Basic Law: Freedom of Religious Belief|religious beliefs or religious law]] as a motivation for the enactment of prohibitions. Estuaria has never had neither {{W|blasphemy law}}s nor {{W|sodomy law}}s (which have historically been used to target homosexuals). Laws prohibiting discriminatory speech in the press and media are some of the strictest in the world. Some consider however that hate speech laws in Estuaria are too broad or severe and damage freedom of speech.

The above statement is a result of Estuaria's longstanding history of [[W:Religious pluralism|religious pluralism]] and the strong Baliscan adherence to a policy of ''{{W|laïcité|laïcitat}}'' since the [[Anyos Federales|''Anyos Federales'']]. ''Laïcitat'', also known as "Baliscan secularism", vehemently opposes the influence of religion in politics and more broadly in public spheres, and prohibits government influence in religious spheres. However the policy does allow for the government to step in if the rights of a citizen are: a) infringed upon by a religious group; b) suppressed or taken away; c) are abused in a way as to stir unrest or infringe on the rights of another citizen of a different faith.

