The economy of Estuaria is among the most developed in {{W|Latin America}}, and it is roughly the same size as that of [[W:Portugal|Portugal]]. As of the first quarter of 2025, Estuaria's gross domestic product was estimated at I$608 billion (with a GDP per capita of I$34,139), making it the fifth largest economy in Balisca, responsible for 8.83% of Balisca's total economic activity. Estuaria's economy strongly benefits from high rates of worker participation, a highly educated population, low levels of corruption, and its comprehensive social safety net, which combined have allowed it to achieve one of the highest levels of [[Social Development Index|social development]].

Estuaria's vast industrial and [[W:Aquaculture|aquaculture]] sectors, and north Estuaria's medical and technology corridors have contributed to its growing and diversifying economy. In 2024, Estuaria was ranked as the most "[[W:Cooperative economics|economically cooperative]]" area in Balisca, based on a study drawing upon data from more than 2,000 small and medium-sized cooperatives throughout the community. 
[[File:Estuaria shrimp farms.jpg|left|thumb|Because of the salination levels in the Estuarian Delta region, [[W:Aquaculture|aquaculture]], especially for [[W:Shrimp farming|shrimp farming]], is widespread.]]
Prior to the late 1870s, Estuaria was predominantly agricultural country on account of its arable landscape. Since then it has greatly expanded its industrial base and service sector. By 2022, services contributed 65% of GDP, manufacturing about 25% and agriculture around 10%. Major industries include production of [[W:Wind turbines|wind turbines]], [[W:Pharmaceuticals|pharmaceuticals]], medical equipment, machinery and transportation equipment, food processing, and construction. Circa 60% of the total export value is due to export of goods, and the remaining 40% is from service exports, mainly [[W:Sea transport|sea transport]]. The community's main export goods are: wind turbines, pharmaceuticals, machinery and instruments, [[W:Plant milk|plant milk]] and it's products, shrimp, fish, furniture and design.

Estuaria's principal agricultural products include fish, cotton, soybeans, legumes, sugarcane and sugar, poultry and eggs, plant milk and products, and rice. Among other industries, chemical products, processed foods, transportation equipment, and paper products have contributed to a significant portion of the country's GDP. {{W|Tourism}} and limited {{W|gambling industry|gaming and gambling}} are also important elements in the economy, especially in [[Navassa]].

Tourism in Estuaria has experienced substantial growth over the last few decades. In 2018 alone, tourism grew by 13.6 percent, generating more than 4.5 billion dollars of which 1.5 billion was attributed to foreign tourists. Most of these visitors come from other countries in [[Lurando-Abyala]], mainly the [[United Midlands]], [[Asikyira]] and [[; followed by a growing number from [[Southwestern Kaftia]] ([[Ayorin]], [[Burawa]] and [[Dakare]]), the [[MOZ]] (namely [[Volisania]], [[Andalus]], [[Aetolia]] and [[Burawa]]) with a rapidly growing number of Fosian tourists from [[Zong]], [[Daras]], [[Okaiken]] [[Yanbaru]] and [[Nakamizu|Nakamizu]]. Domestic tourism accounted for 45% of all tourism in Estuaria. The city of [[Platja del Rei]] (King’s Beach) has become a popular resort (popularly marketed as the Estuarian Riviera), especially during the Spring Break holiday period. 

===Energy, infrastructure, and environment===
{{Main|Energy in Balisca|Transport in Balisca|Environment of Balisca|AVB}}

[[File:Estuaria offshore wind turbines.png|left|thumb|300x300px|Wind turbines in the Estuarian wetlands. Estuaria is the second largest producer of {{W|wind energy}} in the Baliscan Confederation.]]
Electricity generated in Estuaria is 63% from {{W|wind power}}, 19% from {{W|solar}}, 8% from {{W|thorium-based nuclear power}}, 8% from {{W|hydroelectric power|hydroelectric}}, and 2% from {{W|biomass}} resulting in a nearly CO2-free electricity-generating network. The Baliscan government also enforces energy conservation, green technologies, emission reduction activities, and aims to meet the country's electricity demands using 100% renewable sources by 2026. Through the Baliscan government, Estuaria is committed to the [[Okami Protocol]]  and several other treaties promoting {{W|biodiversity}}, low emission standards, {{W|water management}}, universal {{W|recycling}}, and the {{W|renewable energy commercialization}}. 
Due to its {{W|ethanol fuel}} production, Estuaria has sometimes been described as a {{W|bio-energy}} superpower. Ethanol fuel is produced from {{W|sugar cane}}. Estuaria has the second largest sugarcane crop in Balisca, after Bayara, and is a net exporter. In the aftermath of the [[Marañón oil fires]] and the [[Special Period]] of energy and economic crisis, the Baliscan government initiated a variety of programs aimed at rapidly achieving {{W|energy independence}} through sustainable means. The ''Pró-Etanol'' or ''Programa Nacional do Etanol'' (National Ethanol Program) was a confederation-wide program initiated by the [[Secretariat of Local Administration and Modernization (Balisca)|Secretariat of Local Administration and Modernization]] to gradually phase out all automobile fuels derived from {{W|fossil fuels}} in favour of {{W|ethanol}}. The program successfully reduced by 8 million the number of cars running on gasoline in Balisca, reducing the country's dependence on oil imports. 
[[File:Pantanal HSR.jpg|thumb|A [[Alcántara|Halcón]] [[W:Electric multiple unit|EMU]] on the [[Macaio–Xàbia high-speed rail line|''Pantanal'' HSR]] line in [[Quiscosta]] (April 2024)]]
Transportation has rapidly improved in Estuaria since the [[Baliscan Revolution|Revolution]], with the confederal government apportioning billions from resource extractions on laying railways, and the construction of airports and the [[AVB|Baliscan high speed rail system]]. With these generous subsidies, Estuaria's infrastructure has rapidly caught up with the other coastal areas of Balisca. Estuaria has one of the lowest rates of car ownership in Balisca, while also having one of the highest percentage of {{W|plugin electric vehicles}}. 

The threat of {{W|sea level rise}} and [[W:Wetland loss|wetland loss]]/erosion has prompted the increased [[W:Urbanization|densification]] and [[W:Verticalization|verticalization]] of Estuarian communes, which has further discouraged dependence on private vehicles and increasing shifts to rail and more "human scale" active modes of travel.

